// arXiv source page 1
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 790.056pt, "1 ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 18pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(169.1pt, 88.94pt, "Nanoscale Magnonic Neurons ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(190.61pt, 131.78pt, "K. G. Fripp, A. V. Shytov and V. V. Kruglyak ")
  #line-at(127.1pt, 165.02pt, "University of Exeter, Stocker Road, Exeter, EX4 4QL, United Kingdom ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 198.38pt, "We use micromagnetic simulations to demonstrate neuron functionality of two-dimensional ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 217.34pt, "(2D) chiral magnonic resonators.  Our design exploits nonlinear resonant scattering of spin ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 236.3pt, "waves propagating in a YIG medium from an edge mode of a permalloy nano-element.  The ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 255.26pt, "reduced frequency and volume of the edge mode facilitate matching it to the YIG modes and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 274.25pt, "give rise to their wide-angle scattering.  As the amplitudes of the incident spin waves increase, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 293.21pt, "the edge mode exhibits a positive nonlinear frequency shift.  This shift leads to a complex ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 312.17pt, "frequency-dependent nonlinear variation of the amplitude and phase of spin waves scattered ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.13pt, "in different directions.  We show that the scattered waves are strong enough to “activate” ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 350.09pt, "secondary neurons.  This provides the connectivity required for combining our proposed ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 369.17pt, "neurons into 2D magnonic neural networks.   ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 790.056pt, "2 ")
  #line-at(100.34pt, 82.32pt, "The rising significance of research in machine learning and artificial neural networks for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 101.3pt, "modern society was recently recognized by the award of the 2024 Nobel Prize in Physics.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 120.26pt, "Yet, the advent of the artificial intelligence (AI) brings about not only benefits but also the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 139.22pt, "challenge of mounting energy costs of implementing AI with conventional, CMOS-based ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 158.18pt, "computers.  Magnonics [1] might offer a viable alternative hardware platform for energy-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 177.26pt, "efficient neuromorphic and, more generally, unconventional computing.  This would use spin ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 196.22pt, "waves and their quanta (magnons) [2,3]) as information carriers that feature strong ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 215.18pt, "nonlinearity, modest propagation loss, and sub-micrometre wavelengths at GHz frequencies ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 234.14pt, "[1-5].  These properties have been used to build complex nonlinear reservoirs underpinning ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 253.1pt, "various forms of magnonic reservoir computing [4-8], showing different levels of trainability ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 272.09pt, "and success in common benchmark tasks.   ")
  #line-at(100.34pt, 297.05pt, "An alternative, bottom-up approach involves devising a magnonic neuron first and then ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 316.01pt, "using it as a building block of more complex artificial neural networks.  In Ref. [9], we explored ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 334.97pt, "nonlinear properties of chiral magnonic resonators [10-12] in view of their potential ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 354.05pt, "applications as artificial “neurons”. However, only a one-dimensional (1D) system was ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 373.01pt, "considered under assumption of the incident and transmitted spin wave signals propagating ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 391.97pt, "along the same direction. This would limit options for connecting the neuron to those in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 410.93pt, "subsequent layers.  Moreover, the need to match the frequency of the propagating modes to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 429.89pt, "those confined in the resonator led us to using the same material (Permalloy) for both the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 448.87pt, "resonator and the magnonic medium. Yet, construction of large neural networks with a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 467.83pt, "competitive computing efficiency dictates the use of low-damping materials, e.g. yttrium-iron ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 486.79pt, "garnet (YIG) [13,14], for the magnonic medium [15,16].   ")
  #line-at(100.34pt, 511.75pt, "In this Letter, we use micromagnetic simulations to demonstrate neuron functionality of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 530.83pt, "two-dimensional (2D) chiral magnonic resonators.  Such neurons exploit nonlinear resonant ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 549.79pt, "scattering of spin waves propagating in a YIG medium from an edge mode of a permalloy disk ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 568.75pt, "nano-element.  The reduced frequency and volume of the edge mode [17,18] facilitate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 587.71pt, "matching it to the YIG modes and give rise to their wide-angle scattering.  As the amplitudes ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 606.67pt, "of the incident spin waves increase, the edge mode exhibits a positive nonlinear frequency ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 625.66pt, "shift.  This shift leads to a complex frequency-dependent nonlinear variation of the amplitude ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 644.62pt, "and phase of spin waves scattered in different directions.  We show that the scattered waves ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 663.58pt, "have amplitude sufficient to “activate” secondary neurons.  This provides the connectivity ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 682.54pt, "required for combining our proposed neurons into 2D magnonic neural networks [5,12].   ")
]

